Summary Judgment
After settlement, summary judgment is the most common way that federal civil actions reach a conclusion. Summary judgment is the primary means by which courts resolve the merits of civil actions. What is summary judgment? It is a form of pretrial judgment, in which the court considers the evidence and legal arguments presented by the parties. As the name suggests, summary judgment exists as a means by which a court can decide the merits of a case without the time and expense of a trial. Instead, if the court determines that summary judgment is appropriate, the court enters a judgment in the case without ever holding a trial.

Summary judgment is unlike a dismissal under Rule 12(b)(6), and it is unlike a judgment entered after trial. There are two crucial differences. First, the evidence considered is different. Unlike motions to dismiss for failure to state a claim (under Rule 12), which require the court to accept as true all factual allegations in the complaint, a motion for summary judgment (under Rule 56, below) has the parties present evidence—documents, affidavits, deposition transcripts, answers to requests to admit, and so on—that tends to support or refute the claims in the case. Unlike trial, there is no live testimony (only deposition transcripts). At summary judgment, the court considers only the “paper” record created by the parties.

Second, the standard for granting a motion for summary judgment is different from the standards for a Rule 12(b)(6) motion or for granting a verdict after trial. At the pleadings stage, the question is not whether the plaintiff has proved or failed to prove its case, but merely whether the plaintiff has pleaded allegations that make it plausible that the plaintiff would win at trial. At trial, the finder of fact—the jury, or in the absence of a jury, the judge—weighs all of the evidence and, depending on whose evidence is stronger, renders a verdict in favor of one side or the other. Either way, judgment is entered.

Not so for summary judgment. The question at summary judgment is, very roughly speaking, whether the evidence is so lopsided that a trial would be a waste of time. If it is, then summary judgment in favor of the party destined to win at trial is appropriate. But if there is doubt about what the evidence proves, then neither side gets summary judgment. Instead, the case proceeds to trial.

To be clear, “lopsided” and “waste of time” are not legal standards. Rule 56(a), however, is a legal standard.

564
Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 56
Summary Judgment

(a) Motion for Summary Judgment or Partial Summary Judgment. A party may move for summary judgment, identifying each claim or defense—or the part of each claim or defense—on which summary judgment is sought. The court shall grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The court should state on the record the reasons for granting or denying the motion.


